  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 PM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\n- Cooler Midweek Then Warming Up Into The Weekend\n\nScattered showers and isolated thunderstorm cells will persist \ninto the evening amid breezy northwest winds and cold air \nadvection. On Wednesday, 850 mb temperatures around 8 C will keep \nhighs no warmer than the mid 70s even under mostly sunny skies, \nour coolest day since June 26.\n\nThe large-scale wave pattern remains rather steady-state for the \nrest of the month, with an upper-level high over Colorado and the \nSouthwest, and troughing over the eastern Great Lakes. Shortwaves \nwill occasionally traverse this flow regime. Heat is expected to \nbuild out west and then wash over Michigan this weekend. Ensembles\nare nearly unanimous on the warming trend Thursday into Sunday, \nthen diverge from Monday onward. Temperatures are favored to climb\nto around 90 on Sunday with the higher and more uncomfortable dew\npoints also arriving on that day.\n\n- Chance of Thunderstorms Sunday-Monday\n\nAlong with the arrival of warmer and more humid air on Sunday, \nsteeper midlevel lapse rates advecting over the region will also \nserve to increase instability. Another fairly robust shortwave \ntrough and associated surface low moving across the northern Great\nLakes will provide the next chance of thunderstorms between \nSunday and Monday. Meaningful differences do exist on the exact \ntrack and arrival time of the low, and that means confidence in \nthe threat heavy rain or severe weather is relatively low for our \narea at this time.",
